# Human-readable documentation

Write docs so a tired teammate understands them on the first read. Prefer concrete scenarios over abstract jargon.

## Style reference

Canonical tone and concreteness: [`docs-zensical/docs/ru/product/overview.md`](docs-zensical/docs/ru/product/overview.md) (EN mirror: `docs-zensical/docs/en/product/overview.md`).

Match that level of clarity: say what the person does, what the system does next, and where the data goes.

## Do

- Explain with a real flow: who → what happens → what they see next
- Prefer everyday words; if a term is needed, unpack it in the same sentence
- Name the actual thing: «на странице Приглашения», «после принятия приглашения контакты открываются этой компании»
- Keep sentences short; one idea per sentence when possible
- For tables: promise in the left column, plain meaning in the right — not slogans
- Keep product UI strings in Russian, in quotes, in both locales

## Don't

- Vague abstractions without a scenario
- Buzzword stacks that do not say what the user experiences
- Assuming the reader already knows proctoring, a pattern, or a one-off offer — define in plain words on first use in the page
- Writing like a pitch deck when the page is a product overview
- Inventing requirements that are not in the BRD

## Quick check before finishing

1. Can someone outside the team retell the paragraph in their own words?
2. Is there a concrete actor (candidate, employer, admin) or only “the system”?
3. Did you say where data goes (resume → form → employer card) instead of only “sync”?
